# What survives when a paper leaves the lab: re-testing {{papers}} robot-learning papers on a €{{rig_eur}} rig

*Ravindu Somawansa, Shisso Robotics. Draft, not submitted.*

## Abstract

We re-tested {{c_paper}} claims from {{papers}} robot-learning papers on a cheap rig, and {{c_paper_repro}} of them held. The rig is an SO-101 arm with a Jetson Orin NX, and its parts cost €{{rig_eur}}. Most of the papers were shown in simulation or on far more expensive hardware, so we wanted to know what survives when a claim leaves the lab. For each claim we committed the bar before the test and ran the cheapest baseline first, on the same rows. That baseline matters, because it is often what a method loses to. The failures fall into three classes that we propose: methods too slow for the edge GPU, zero-shot judges that lose to the episode length, and latent planners that lose to the commanded joints. Every number in the paper can be rebuilt from the public repository.

## 1. Introduction

Most robot-learning papers are shown in simulation or on expensive rigs. Of the {{papers}} papers in this study, {{rig_far}} were shown in simulation or on a rig above $20k, and {{rig_lt5k}} on a setup under $5k like ours. A small team that wants to use these methods has to guess which claims still hold on its own hardware. We built the register so that we would not have to guess.

The register has one row per claim. Each row keeps the claim in the paper's words, our bar, our result and the rows behind it. It grew out of our own write-ups: each re-test was published with its data when it ran, and there are {{l0}} write-ups so far.

Take one row. An adaptive chunking paper claims that choosing the chunk length from the entropy of sampled chunks follows the phases of the task, with long chunks in transport and short ones at the grasp. To choose one chunk length the method samples many chunks, and on our edge GPU this takes {{ex_a_ours}}. Our real-time budget is {{ex_a_bar}}. So the row records a fail on this rig, and it says nothing about the method on the hardware it was shown on.

Over the whole register, {{c_paper_repro}} of {{c_paper}} claims hold and {{c_paper_fail}} fail. Several of the failures are not near misses. In those rows the learned method loses to a baseline with no learning in it, and we think this is the main lesson of the register.

## 2. How a row is made

Each row starts with a bar committed to version control before the test runs. We do this because a bar written after the result tends to drift toward it, even without intent. Section 5 checks from the commit history how well we kept this rule.

Before testing the learned method, we run the cheapest signal it has to beat, on the same rows. For a model that predicts the next state, this is copy-last: the next state is taken to be the current one. For a learned latent it is the commanded joints, because the state of the arm is already in them. For a judge of success it is the length of the episode, which on this rig already says a lot about the outcome. These baselines take minutes to run, and when a method loses to one of them, its bar no longer matters much.

We chose the papers for the stack this rig runs: world models, VLA policies, failure monitors, chunking and edge latency. So the failure rate describes this register, not the field. The register file lists every tested paper and every paper scheduled next, each entered before its test, so a paper cannot be dropped after its result is known.

