## 4. Three failure classes

The adaptive chunking row fails on time. The method may work on a faster GPU, but on the edge GPU it misses the real-time budget. We call this class A, the clock. It is the easiest class to avoid, because a method can be timed on the target device before anything else is built.

Class B is about judges. Used without any training on this rig, a learned reward model detected failures with an AUROC of {{ex_b_ours}}. The episode length alone reached {{ex_b_len}}. We put in class B every judge of success, failure or progress that loses to such a trivial signal. Our guess, which we have not tested, is that a zero-shot judge has never seen this rig, while the length of an episode is measured on it.

Class C is about learned latents. A world model ranked the executed chunk first in {{ex_c_ours}} of anchors, below its bar of {{ex_c_bar}}, while an action-only baseline reached {{ex_c_base}}. Class C holds the latents used to plan, rank or track progress that lose to the joints, to copy-last or to an action-only baseline. On a small arm the commanded joints already describe much of the state, so a latent has to add something the joints do not have, and in these rows it did not.

These rows illustrate the classes and do not count them. The classes are our proposal, from the author's reading of the {{fail_rows}} failed claim rows, and a second reader is needed to know if they hold. For someone with a similar rig, they give an order for checking a paper: time it on the device, then compare it with the trivial baseline on the same rows, and only then look at its bar.

## 5. How far the register can be trusted

A verdict means something only if its bar came before its result. Notes and commit dates can be edited by hand. Commit ancestry cannot, unless one rewrites history that others have already cloned. So for each gate we find the first commit that contains its bar and the first that contains its result, and compare them (`repro/prereg-check/scripts/prereg_audit.py`, abridged):

```python
def ancestor(a, b):
    return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(RR), "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", a, b]).returncode == 0
...
            elif b[0] == res[0]:
                cls = "same-commit"
            elif ancestor(b[0], res[0]):
                cls = "bar-first"
            elif ancestor(res[0], b[0]):
                cls = "result-first"
```

Table 1 gives the class of each of the {{pr_rows}} gates that ran and were logged by 2026-10-31. Most are bar-first, and {{pr_result_first}} are result-first. A same-commit gate is one whose bar and result entered the repository together, so it tells nothing about which came first. Text search alone first found {{v1_same_commit}} same-commit gates, mostly from batch logging. We went back to the earlier files that hold their bars, and {{sc_to_bf}} of them moved to bar-first and {{sc_to_nb}} to no-bar. The audit table in the repository gives the evidence for each move.

Table 1: Commit-order class of each gate. The audited column is the one the paper uses; the first pass is what text search alone gave on the same gates.

| class | first text pass | audited, 2026-10-31 | audited, today |
|---|---|---|---|
| bar-first | {{v1_bar_first}} | {{pr_bar_first}} | {{pt_bar_first}} |
| same-commit | {{v1_same_commit}} | {{pr_same_commit}} | {{pt_same_commit}} |
| result-first | — | {{pr_result_first}} | {{pt_result_first}} |
| no bar | {{v1_no_bar}} | {{pr_no_bar}} | {{pt_no_bar}} |
| unresolved | {{v1_unresolved}} | — | — |
| gates | {{pr_rows}} | {{pr_rows}} | {{pr_today}} |

The early gates mixed point estimates and different intervals. We recomputed {{rc_rows}} of them from the stored rows with one statistic, the episode bootstrap ({{rc_resamples}} resamples, percentile 95 % CI). For {{rc_flips}} gates the bar falls inside the interval (Table 2), so we mark their verdicts as fragile but keep the pre-registered point verdicts. The false-intervention threshold was fitted to meet its rate, so its interval is only descriptive.

Table 2: The three gates whose verdict flips somewhere inside its 95 % interval.

| gate | n | estimate | 95 % CI | bar | point verdict |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| colour judge (no learning), accuracy | {{acc_n}} | {{acc_est}} % | {{acc_lo}}–{{acc_hi}} % | ≥ {{acc_bar}} % | {{acc_verdict}} |
| oscillation among failed episodes | {{osc_n}} | {{osc_est}} % | {{osc_lo}}–{{osc_hi}} % | ≥ {{osc_bar}} % | {{osc_verdict}} |
| judge threshold, false interventions | {{tau_n}} | {{tau_est}} % | {{tau_lo}}–{{tau_hi}} % | ≤ {{tau_bar}} % | {{tau_verdict}} |

All {{l0}} write-ups rebuild from their published rows. The check for the cold reruns prints:

```text
$ python3 repro/cold-reproduction/l0_check.py
ok  posts: 29 (report 29)
ok  L0 PASS: 29 (report 29)
ok  L1 REPRO: 6 (report 6)
ok  L1 DIFF: 0 (report 0)
ok  L1 classes: {'REPRO': 6, '—': 3, 'SKIP': 8, 'FAIL-ENV': 12}
L0 PASS
```

We ran the cold reruns from a fresh clone with no credentials. {{l1_repro}} gates reran and matched, {{l1_exact}} of them exactly. {{l1_env}} more stop at the world-model environment, which is not published yet. The remaining {{l1_skip}} need a larger GPU, a private checkpoint or the arm, or a step the write-up leaves out. We also checked the citations and paper-side fields of the register against the papers' PDFs, and corrected them. The register names {{ps_ids}} arXiv ids. The ids missing from our mirror of the papers all belong to scheduled tests, and one tested paper has no arXiv id, so it could not be checked against a PDF.

## 6. Limitations

The largest risk is observer bias. One operator, the author, ran the rig and labelled every outcome, with the arm at rest but not always blind to it. Where the commit order is known, the bar predates the run, so a label could not move the bar. For {{pr_unprov}} of the {{pr_rows}} gates the order is not known, and {{sc_to_bf}} bar-first verdicts rest on matching the bar text in an earlier file. A blind second reader would show whether verdicts flip. Under the bootstrap {{rc_flips}} verdicts are fragile, and the cold reruns cover {{l1_repro}} gates so far, because the world-model environment is not published yet.

## 7. Related work

Other work on this arm ranks policies. A real-world benchmark reports a taxonomy of failures and recoveries for VLA policies [@2606.08881], and a fleet of SO-101 cells evaluates policies at scale [@2607.24481]. In simulation, vla-eval runs VLAs across 14 benchmarks [@2603.13966], and a benchmark of demonstration quality studies one method family [@2610.18869]. We do not rank policies. We take the claim a paper makes about its method, fix a bar for it, and test it on one cheap rig. The closest work is RoboSeed [@2610.40026]. It scores VLA and world-action models on five dimensions, among them reproduction evidence and deployment affordability. Its reproduction score counts audit milestones such as configuration mapping, task records and seeds, and its authors say the score is not a probability that a result is correct. A register answers a narrower question, whether a claim holds on one rig, and it gives the rows to check the answer.

## 8. Conclusion

> **Conclusion.** We re-tested {{c_paper}} claims from {{papers}} robot-learning papers on a €{{rig_eur}} rig with ${{cost}} of compute, and {{c_paper_repro}} held. Several methods lost to a baseline with no learning in it: copy-last, the commanded joints or the episode length. So our advice to anyone re-testing a paper on a cheap rig is to run that baseline first, on the same rows, with the bar committed before the run. Every row is published, and every number here can be rebuilt from the repository (`github.com/ravediamond/paper-register-reproducibility`). We will keep adding rows as the scheduled tests run.

